The Normal Distribution equation
\[\begin{equation}
     f(x; \mu, \sigma) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi\sigma^{2}}}
     e^{-(x-\mu)^{2}/(2\sigma^{2})},
     -\infty &lt; x &lt; \infty
  \end{equation}\] - There are two parameters, \(\mu\) and \(\sigma\) - There is an entire family of normal distributions - It can be shown that \(E(x)=\mu\) and \(V(X)=\sigma^{2}\) , so the parameters are the mean and the standard deviation of \(X\) - The statement that \(X\) is normally distributed with parameters \(\mu\) and \(\sigma^{2}\) is often abbreviated \(X \sim N(\mu, \sigma^{2})\)

